// These are model instructions, not an execution interceptor. The native
// Runtime continues to own command execution, sandboxing and approvals.
const webRetrievalInstructions = "For online information retrieval, use the applicable selected retrieval connector or native web retrieval. Once either retrieval route is available or has been used for an information need, do not use exec_command or any shell/local script to search, scrape, fetch, validate, or supplement web pages for that need. This includes Python urllib/requests, curl, wget, and Node HTTP/fetch, regardless of the current permission mode. Do not request broader local network permissions to work around a retrieval limitation. If page content is needed, use only the applicable exposed retrieval tools; never assume a search result includes the full page. If that route has no page-reading tool or cannot obtain the content, state what evidence is missing and answer from the available sources without a local scraping fallback. Local computation, processing already obtained data, and commands genuinely required for a user-requested software or file task remain allowed; those tasks do not justify web-scraping fallbacks. " +
	"On the first permission, network, DNS, proxy, authentication, or other retrieval failure, explain the observed cause and its effect in the user's language before continuing. Distinguish an observed DNS error from a proven sandbox denial; do not invent a root cause. Do not repeat the same or equivalent failed operation while the relevant conditions are unchanged, even if the process exited with code 0. Catching an exception and printing it is still a failed retrieval, not successful page access. Changing interpreters, command wrappers, query spacing, or unrelated arguments is not a changed condition. Retry only when there is evidence of a relevant correction, restored service/access, or an explicit user request to retry, and still follow the retrieval-route restriction. A different relevant source may be read through the applicable retrieval route only when the failure was source-specific; do not cycle through URLs when the route itself is unavailable. If no permitted useful retrieval remains, explain the limitation once and finish with supported facts. "
